insight:
Here's an updated version of the script that includes the URL parameter:



  var urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
  var name = urlParams.get("name");
  var email = urlParams.get("email");
  var phone = urlParams.get("phone");


To parse the query string and extract the values of each parameter, we use the URLSearchParams object. The get() method returns the value associated with the specified parameter name, or null if no such parameter was found.  Note that the parameter names need to be included in the URL, preceded by an equal sign (=), e.g. https://example.com/?name=John&email=john@gmail.com&phone=1234567.

Sure, here is a modified version of the previous JavaScript script that includes the desired URL parameter:



  window.location.href += "?name=John&email=john@gmail.com&phone=1234567";


The += operator is used to concatenate strings, in this case adding the desired query parameters to the existing URL.  Be aware though that there may be security implications when using query parameters in this way - they are typically visible to anyone who views the web browser history or network traffic.  A better approach would be to use session cookies or local storage instead.
